# Glossary for Introductory Organic and Biological Chemistry for FCC (CHEM 3B).
# Each entry: (words — first is the headword, others are synonyms the pop-up should also match, definition)
# Definitions written by Dr. Dan (Daniel Kwasniewski), Fresno City College, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0.
G = [
# ---------- Chapter 1: bonding review and intermolecular forces ----------
("Lewis structure", "A drawing of a molecule that shows every valence electron: shared pairs as bonds (lines) and unshared pairs as dots."),
("lone pair, nonbonding pair", "A pair of valence electrons that belongs to one atom and is not shared in a bond. Lone pairs on O and N take part in hydrogen bonding and make amines basic."),
("octet rule", "The tendency of atoms in molecules to have eight valence electrons around them (two for hydrogen) by sharing or transferring electrons."),
("covalent bond", "A bond formed when two atoms share a pair of electrons. A double bond shares two pairs and a triple bond shares three."),
("electronegativity", "The ability of an atom in a bond to pull shared electrons toward itself. F, O, N and Cl are the most electronegative atoms in this course."),
("polar covalent bond, bond polarity", "A covalent bond in which electrons are shared unequally because the two atoms differ in electronegativity, giving one end a partial negative charge and the other a partial positive charge."),
("VSEPR theory", "Valence shell electron pair repulsion: electron groups around a central atom spread as far apart as possible, which predicts the shape of the molecule."),
("molecular geometry", "The three-dimensional arrangement of the atoms in a molecule, such as linear, trigonal planar, tetrahedral, trigonal pyramidal or bent."),
("tetrahedral", "The shape around an atom with four electron groups and no lone pairs, with bond angles of about 109.5°. Every carbon with four single bonds is tetrahedral."),
("trigonal planar", "The flat shape around an atom with three electron groups and no lone pairs, with bond angles of 120°, as at a C=C or C=O carbon."),
("polar molecule, molecular polarity", "A molecule with an overall dipole because its polar bonds do not cancel; polar molecules attract each other and dissolve in water."),
("intermolecular forces, IMF", "Attractions between separate molecules (dispersion forces, dipole–dipole attractions and hydrogen bonds) that determine boiling point, melting point and solubility."),
("London dispersion forces, dispersion forces", "The weak attractions between all molecules caused by temporary shifts in their electron clouds; the only intermolecular force between nonpolar molecules, stronger for larger molecules."),
("dipole–dipole attraction, dipole-dipole interaction", "The attraction between the partial positive end of one polar molecule and the partial negative end of another."),
("hydrogen bonding, hydrogen bond", "A strong dipole–dipole attraction between an H bonded to O, N or F and a lone pair on another O, N or F. It explains the high boiling points of alcohols, carboxylic acids and water, and holds DNA and proteins in shape."),
("like dissolves like", "The rule that polar and ionic substances dissolve in polar solvents such as water, and nonpolar substances dissolve in nonpolar solvents."),
("hydrophilic", "Water-loving: polar or ionic, and attracted to water."),
("hydrophobic", "Water-fearing: nonpolar, and excluded from water; hydrophobic groups cluster together in water."),
# ---------- Chapter 2: alkanes ----------
("organic chemistry", "The chemistry of carbon compounds."),
("hydrocarbon", "A compound that contains only carbon and hydrogen."),
("saturated hydrocarbon", "A hydrocarbon with only single bonds, holding the maximum number of hydrogen atoms; alkanes and cycloalkanes are saturated."),
("alkane", "A saturated hydrocarbon with the general formula C<sub>n</sub>H<sub>2n+2</sub>, such as methane, propane and octane."),
("cycloalkane", "A saturated hydrocarbon in which the carbon atoms form a ring, such as cyclohexane."),
("homologous series", "A series of compounds that differ from one another by a CH<sub>2</sub> unit, such as methane, ethane, propane and butane."),
("functional group", "The atom or group of atoms in a molecule that gives the family its characteristic reactions, such as the –OH of an alcohol or the C=O of a ketone."),
("molecular formula", "A formula that gives the number of each kind of atom in a molecule, such as C<sub>4</sub>H<sub>10</sub>, without showing how they are connected."),
("expanded structural formula", "A drawing that shows every atom and every bond in a molecule."),
("condensed structural formula", "A formula that groups each carbon with its hydrogens, such as CH<sub>3</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>3</sub>."),
("skeletal structure, line-angle structure, line structure", "A drawing in which carbon atoms are the corners and line ends, hydrogens on carbon are not shown, and all other atoms are written out."),
("isomers", "Compounds with the same molecular formula but different structures or arrangements."),
("constitutional isomers, structural isomers", "Isomers whose atoms are connected in a different order, such as butane and 2-methylpropane."),
("conformation, conformations", "The different shapes a molecule takes by rotating around single bonds; conformations are not different compounds."),
("alkyl group", "An alkane with one hydrogen removed, attached as a branch, such as methyl (–CH<sub>3</sub>), ethyl and isopropyl."),
("IUPAC name, IUPAC system", "The systematic naming system of the International Union of Pure and Applied Chemistry: find the longest chain, number it, then name and locate the branches and functional groups."),
("primary carbon, 1° carbon", "A carbon bonded to one other carbon atom."),
("secondary carbon, 2° carbon", "A carbon bonded to two other carbon atoms."),
("tertiary carbon, 3° carbon", "A carbon bonded to three other carbon atoms."),
("combustion", "Burning: the reaction of a hydrocarbon with O<sub>2</sub> to give CO<sub>2</sub> and H<sub>2</sub>O and release energy. With too little oxygen it also produces carbon monoxide."),
("halogenation", "A reaction that adds halogen atoms to a molecule; in alkanes, a substitution that replaces H with Cl or Br, and in alkenes, an addition across the double bond."),
("substitution reaction", "A reaction in which one atom or group replaces another, as when Cl replaces H on an alkane."),
("haloalkane, alkyl halide", "An alkane in which a halogen (F, Cl, Br or I) replaces one or more hydrogens; many inhaled anesthetics are halogenated compounds."),
# ---------- Chapter 3: alkenes, alkynes, aromatics ----------
("unsaturated hydrocarbon", "A hydrocarbon with one or more double or triple bonds, holding fewer hydrogens than the matching alkane."),
("alkene", "A hydrocarbon with a carbon–carbon double bond; names end in -ene."),
("alkyne", "A hydrocarbon with a carbon–carbon triple bond; names end in -yne."),
("cis–trans isomers, cis-trans isomers, geometric isomers", "Stereoisomers that differ in which side of a double bond (or ring) their groups are on: cis on the same side, trans on opposite sides."),
("cis", "Describes two groups on the same side of a double bond or ring."),
("trans", "Describes two groups on opposite sides of a double bond or ring."),
("addition reaction", "A reaction in which atoms add across a double or triple bond, converting it to a single (or double) bond."),
("hydrogenation", "The addition of H<sub>2</sub> across a double or triple bond, using a metal catalyst such as Ni or Pt."),
("hydration", "The addition of water (H and OH) across a double bond to make an alcohol, using an acid catalyst."),
("hydrohalogenation", "The addition of HCl or HBr across a double bond to give a haloalkane."),
("Markovnikov's rule, Markovnikov’s rule", "When H–X or H–OH adds to an unsymmetrical alkene, the H goes to the double-bond carbon that already has more hydrogens."),
("polymer", "A very large molecule made of many repeating small units (monomers)."),
("monomer", "A small molecule that links with many others to build a polymer."),
("addition polymer", "A polymer formed by joining alkene monomers through their double bonds, such as polyethylene and polypropylene."),
("aromatic compound, aromatic hydrocarbon", "A compound containing a benzene ring or a similar ring with delocalized electrons; aromatic rings are unusually stable and undergo substitution rather than addition."),
("benzene", "C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>6</sub>: a flat six-carbon ring whose six pi electrons are delocalized evenly around the ring, often drawn as a hexagon with a circle inside."),
("phenyl group", "A benzene ring attached as a substituent, –C<sub>6</sub>H<sub>5</sub>."),
("ortho, meta, para", "Prefixes for the positions of two groups on a benzene ring: ortho (o-) adjacent, meta (m-) separated by one carbon, para (p-) opposite."),
("polynuclear aromatic hydrocarbons, PAHs", "Compounds of fused benzene rings, such as naphthalene; several, found in smoke and charred food, are carcinogens."),
# ---------- Chapter 4: alcohols, phenols, ethers, thiols ----------
("alcohol", "A compound with a hydroxyl group (–OH) bonded to a saturated carbon; names end in -ol."),
("hydroxyl group", "The –OH functional group of alcohols and phenols."),
("primary alcohol, 1° alcohol", "An alcohol whose –OH carbon is bonded to one other carbon; it oxidizes to an aldehyde and then to a carboxylic acid."),
("secondary alcohol, 2° alcohol", "An alcohol whose –OH carbon is bonded to two other carbons; it oxidizes to a ketone."),
("tertiary alcohol, 3° alcohol", "An alcohol whose –OH carbon is bonded to three other carbons; it is not oxidized."),
("glycol, diol", "An alcohol with two –OH groups, such as ethylene glycol and propylene glycol."),
("phenol", "A compound with an –OH group bonded directly to a benzene ring; phenols are weak acids and many are antiseptics."),
("ether", "A compound with an oxygen bonded to two carbon groups, R–O–R′; ethers cannot hydrogen bond to each other and are volatile."),
("thiol", "A compound with an –SH (sulfhydryl) group; thiols have strong odors and are oxidized to disulfides."),
("disulfide, disulfide bond", "An –S–S– link formed by oxidizing two thiol groups; disulfide bonds cross-link proteins such as keratin and insulin."),
("dehydration", "A reaction that removes water; an alcohol heated with acid loses H<sub>2</sub>O to give an alkene (or, at lower temperature, an ether)."),
("Zaitsev's rule, Zaitsev’s rule", "In the dehydration of an alcohol, the major product is the more substituted alkene (the one with more carbons attached to the double-bond carbons)."),
("oxidation", "In organic chemistry, a gain of oxygen or a loss of hydrogen (in general, a loss of electrons)."),
("reduction", "In organic chemistry, a gain of hydrogen or a loss of oxygen (in general, a gain of electrons)."),
("alcohol dehydrogenase", "The liver enzyme that oxidizes ethanol to acetaldehyde, using NAD<sup>+</sup>; it also turns methanol into toxic formaldehyde."),
# ---------- Chapter 5: aldehydes, ketones, chirality ----------
("carbonyl group", "A carbon double-bonded to an oxygen, C=O; found in aldehydes, ketones, carboxylic acids, esters and amides."),
("aldehyde", "A compound with a carbonyl group bonded to at least one hydrogen, R–CHO; names end in -al. Aldehydes are easily oxidized."),
("ketone", "A compound with a carbonyl group bonded to two carbon groups, R–CO–R′; names end in -one. Ketones resist oxidation."),
("Tollens' test, Tollens’ test, Tollens' reagent, Tollens’ reagent", "A test for aldehydes: Ag<sup>+</sup> oxidizes the aldehyde and is reduced to a silver mirror. Ketones give no reaction."),
("Benedict's test, Benedict’s test, Benedict's reagent, Benedict’s reagent", "A test for aldehydes and reducing sugars: blue Cu<sup>2+</sup> is reduced to a brick-red precipitate of Cu<sub>2</sub>O."),
("hemiacetal", "A compound with an –OH and an –OR on the same carbon, formed when an alcohol adds to an aldehyde (a hemiketal forms from a ketone). Sugar rings are cyclic hemiacetals."),
("acetal", "A compound with two –OR groups on the same carbon, formed from a hemiacetal and a second alcohol; glycosidic bonds are acetal links."),
("chiral", "Not superimposable on its mirror image, like a left and a right hand."),
("achiral", "Superimposable on its mirror image."),
("chiral carbon, stereocenter, chiral center", "A carbon bonded to four different groups; a molecule with one chiral carbon exists as two enantiomers."),
("stereoisomers", "Isomers with the same atoms connected in the same order but arranged differently in space."),
("enantiomers", "A pair of stereoisomers that are non-superimposable mirror images; they share physical properties but can act very differently in the body."),
("diastereomers", "Stereoisomers that are not mirror images of each other, such as glucose and galactose."),
("racemic mixture", "A 50:50 mixture of two enantiomers."),
("Fischer projection", "A flat drawing of a chiral molecule in which horizontal lines point toward the viewer and vertical lines point away; used for sugars and amino acids."),
("D- and L- system, D and L", "A way to name enantiomers by comparing them with glyceraldehyde: in a Fischer projection, the –OH (or –NH<sub>2</sub>) on the chiral carbon farthest from the carbonyl points right in D and left in L."),
# ---------- Chapter 6: carboxylic acids and esters ----------
("carboxylic acid", "A compound with a carboxyl group, –COOH; names end in -oic acid. Carboxylic acids are weak acids."),
("carboxyl group", "The –COOH functional group: a carbonyl carbon bonded to an –OH."),
("carboxylate ion", "The anion R–COO<sup>−</sup> formed when a carboxylic acid loses its H<sup>+</sup>; at body pH (7.4) carboxylic acids exist mainly as carboxylate ions."),
("carboxylic acid salt", "An ionic compound of a carboxylate ion and a metal ion, such as sodium benzoate; salts are more water-soluble than the acids."),
("pKa", "−log K<sub>a</sub>: a measure of acid strength. The lower the pK<sub>a</sub>, the stronger the acid; when the pH is above the pK<sub>a</sub>, the acid is mostly ionized."),
("weak acid", "An acid that only partly ionizes in water, setting up an equilibrium; carboxylic acids and phenols are weak acids."),
("neutralization", "The reaction of an acid with a base to give a salt and water."),
("ester", "A compound with a carbonyl bonded to an –OR group, R–COO–R′; esters give fruits their odors. Named as alkyl alkanoates, such as ethyl acetate."),
("esterification", "The condensation of a carboxylic acid with an alcohol, using an acid catalyst, to give an ester and water."),
("condensation reaction", "A reaction that joins two molecules and releases a small molecule, usually water; esters, amides, peptides, glycosidic bonds and nucleic acids form this way."),
("hydrolysis", "A reaction that breaks a bond by adding water; the reverse of condensation. Digestion is hydrolysis."),
("acid hydrolysis", "Hydrolysis of an ester or amide with water and an acid catalyst, giving a carboxylic acid and an alcohol (or amine)."),
("saponification", "Hydrolysis of an ester with a strong base such as NaOH, giving an alcohol and a carboxylate salt; saponifying fats makes soap."),
("soap", "A sodium or potassium salt of a long-chain fatty acid; its nonpolar tail dissolves grease and its ionic head dissolves in water."),
("thioester", "An ester of a thiol, R–CO–S–R′, such as acetyl CoA; thioester bonds are high-energy bonds in metabolism."),
("phosphate ester", "An ester formed between phosphoric acid and an alcohol; phosphate esters link nucleotides in DNA and RNA and activate sugars in metabolism."),
("acyl group", "The R–C(=O)– part of a carboxylic acid or its derivatives."),
("lactone", "A cyclic ester."),
# ---------- Chapter 7: amines and amides ----------
("amine", "A compound in which one or more carbon groups replace the hydrogens of ammonia; amines are weak bases."),
("primary amine, 1° amine", "An amine with one carbon group bonded to the nitrogen, R–NH<sub>2</sub>."),
("secondary amine, 2° amine", "An amine with two carbon groups bonded to the nitrogen, R<sub>2</sub>NH."),
("tertiary amine, 3° amine", "An amine with three carbon groups bonded to the nitrogen, R<sub>3</sub>N; it cannot hydrogen bond with itself."),
("ammonium salt, amine salt", "The ionic product of an amine and an acid, such as an amine hydrochloride; many drugs are sold as ammonium salts because they dissolve in water."),
("quaternary ammonium ion", "A nitrogen bonded to four carbon groups with a permanent positive charge, as in choline and some disinfectants."),
("heterocyclic compound", "A ring compound in which at least one ring atom is not carbon, usually N, O or S; nucleic-acid bases and many alkaloids are heterocyclic."),
("alkaloid", "A naturally occurring nitrogen-containing base from plants, such as caffeine, nicotine, morphine and cocaine."),
("amide", "A compound with a carbonyl bonded to a nitrogen, R–CO–NR<sub>2</sub>; amides are neutral (not basic). The peptide bond is an amide bond."),
("amidation", "The condensation of a carboxylic acid with ammonia or an amine to form an amide and water."),
("amide hydrolysis", "Breaking an amide with water and acid (giving a carboxylic acid and an ammonium salt) or with base (giving a carboxylate salt and an amine)."),
("neurotransmitter", "A chemical messenger released by a nerve cell that crosses a synapse and binds a receptor on the next cell, such as acetylcholine, dopamine and serotonin."),
("synapse", "The tiny gap between a nerve cell and the next cell, across which neurotransmitters carry the signal."),
("catecholamine", "A neurotransmitter or hormone built on a catechol (benzene ring with two adjacent –OH groups) and an amine: dopamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine."),
("local anesthetic", "A drug that blocks nerve signals in one area, such as lidocaine; most are amines supplied as hydrochloride salts."),
# ---------- Chapter 8: carbohydrates ----------
("carbohydrate, saccharide", "A polyhydroxy aldehyde or ketone, or a compound that hydrolyzes to one; sugars, starch and cellulose."),
("monosaccharide", "A simple sugar that cannot be hydrolyzed into smaller sugars, such as glucose, galactose and fructose."),
("disaccharide", "A sugar made of two monosaccharides joined by a glycosidic bond, such as maltose, lactose and sucrose."),
("polysaccharide", "A polymer of many monosaccharides, such as starch, glycogen and cellulose."),
("aldose", "A monosaccharide with an aldehyde group."),
("ketose", "A monosaccharide with a ketone group, such as fructose."),
("hexose", "A monosaccharide with six carbons, such as glucose."),
("pentose", "A monosaccharide with five carbons, such as ribose."),
("glucose", "The most important monosaccharide (an aldohexose): blood sugar, the main fuel of cells and the monomer of starch, glycogen and cellulose."),
("fructose", "A ketohexose, the sweetest common sugar, found in fruit and honey and in sucrose."),
("galactose", "An aldohexose that differs from glucose only at carbon 4; part of lactose."),
("Haworth projection", "A drawing of a sugar ring as a flat hexagon or pentagon seen edge-on, with groups pointing up or down from the ring carbons."),
("anomeric carbon", "The carbon that was the carbonyl carbon in the open-chain sugar and becomes the hemiacetal carbon in the ring (C1 of glucose)."),
("anomers, α and β anomers", "The two ring forms of a sugar that differ only at the anomeric carbon: in a Haworth projection of a D-sugar, the –OH points down in the α form and up in the β form."),
("mutarotation", "The interconversion of the α and β ring forms of a sugar in solution through the open-chain form."),
("pyranose", "A six-membered sugar ring containing an oxygen, such as glucopyranose."),
("furanose", "A five-membered sugar ring containing an oxygen, such as fructofuranose and ribofuranose."),
("glycosidic bond", "The acetal link that joins a monosaccharide's anomeric carbon to another sugar (or alcohol) through an oxygen; written α(1→4), β(1→4) and so on."),
("reducing sugar", "A sugar with a free hemiacetal that can open to an aldehyde and be oxidized; it gives a positive Benedict's test. Glucose, maltose and lactose are reducing; sucrose is not."),
("maltose", "A disaccharide of two glucose units joined α(1→4); formed when starch is digested."),
("lactose", "Milk sugar: a disaccharide of galactose and glucose joined β(1→4)."),
("sucrose", "Table sugar: a non-reducing disaccharide of glucose and fructose joined through both anomeric carbons."),
("starch", "The storage polysaccharide of plants: amylose (α(1→4) chains) and amylopectin (also branched at α(1→6))."),
("glycogen", "The storage polysaccharide of animals, stored in liver and muscle: highly branched chains of glucose with α(1→4) links and α(1→6) branches."),
("cellulose", "The structural polysaccharide of plants: glucose joined β(1→4). Humans lack the enzyme to digest it, so it is dietary fiber."),
("sugar alcohol, alditol", "The polyalcohol formed by reducing a sugar's carbonyl group, such as sorbitol and xylitol; xylitol is not fermented by mouth bacteria."),
("lactose intolerance", "Difficulty digesting lactose because of too little lactase; undigested lactose is fermented in the colon, causing gas and cramps."),
("galactosemia", "An inherited disorder in which galactose cannot be converted to glucose; infants must avoid milk sugar."),
# ---------- Chapter 9: lipids ----------
("lipid", "A biomolecule that is insoluble in water but soluble in nonpolar solvents: fats, oils, waxes, phospholipids, sphingolipids and steroids."),
("fatty acid", "A long-chain carboxylic acid, usually with an even number of carbons (12 to 20), that may be saturated or unsaturated."),
("saturated fatty acid", "A fatty acid with no C=C double bonds; its straight chains pack tightly, so saturated fats are solid at room temperature."),
("monounsaturated fatty acid", "A fatty acid with one C=C double bond, such as oleic acid."),
("polyunsaturated fatty acid", "A fatty acid with two or more C=C double bonds, such as linoleic acid."),
("omega-3 fatty acid, omega-6 fatty acid, omega number", "A label for an unsaturated fatty acid that gives the position of the first double bond counted from the CH<sub>3</sub> (omega) end."),
("essential fatty acids", "Linoleic acid (an omega-6) and α-linolenic acid (an omega-3): fatty acids the body cannot make and must get from food."),
("triacylglycerol, triglyceride", "A triester of glycerol and three fatty acids: the storage form of fat in the body."),
("fat", "A triacylglycerol that is solid at room temperature, rich in saturated fatty acids; usually from animals."),
("oil", "A triacylglycerol that is liquid at room temperature, rich in unsaturated (cis) fatty acids; usually from plants or fish."),
("trans fat", "A fat containing trans double bonds, mostly from partial hydrogenation of oils; trans fats raise LDL cholesterol."),
("rancidity, oxidative rancidity, hydrolytic rancidity", "Spoilage of fats: oxidation of C=C bonds into short, bad-smelling aldehydes and acids, or hydrolysis that releases free fatty acids such as butyric acid."),
("wax", "An ester of a long-chain fatty acid and a long-chain alcohol; waxes are waterproof coatings, such as beeswax and lanolin."),
("phospholipid", "A lipid containing a phosphate group, with a polar head and two nonpolar tails; the main component of cell membranes."),
("glycerophospholipid", "A phospholipid built on glycerol: two fatty acids, a phosphate and an amino alcohol such as choline (lecithin) or ethanolamine (cephalin)."),
("lecithin, phosphatidylcholine", "A glycerophospholipid with choline on its phosphate; a membrane lipid, an emulsifier in food and a component of lung surfactant."),
("sphingolipid", "A lipid built on sphingosine (an amino alcohol) instead of glycerol, such as sphingomyelin in the myelin sheath of nerves."),
("glycolipid", "A lipid with a sugar attached, such as cerebrosides and gangliosides; glycolipids act as recognition markers on cell surfaces."),
("amphipathic", "Having both a polar (hydrophilic) part and a nonpolar (hydrophobic) part, like soaps, phospholipids and bile salts."),
("micelle", "A spherical cluster of amphipathic molecules in water with the nonpolar tails inside and the polar heads facing the water."),
("lipid bilayer", "The double layer of phospholipids that forms cell membranes, with the polar heads facing the water on both sides and the tails in the middle."),
("fluid-mosaic model", "The model of the cell membrane as a fluid lipid bilayer with proteins, cholesterol and carbohydrate markers floating in it."),
("diffusion, simple diffusion", "Movement of a substance across a membrane from higher to lower concentration without help; small nonpolar molecules such as O<sub>2</sub> cross this way."),
("facilitated transport, facilitated diffusion", "Movement down a concentration gradient through a protein channel or carrier, with no energy needed; glucose enters cells this way."),
("active transport", "Movement of a substance against its concentration gradient through a protein pump, using energy from ATP, such as the Na<sup>+</sup>/K<sup>+</sup> pump."),
("steroid", "A lipid built on four fused rings (three six-membered and one five-membered), such as cholesterol, bile salts and the steroid hormones."),
("cholesterol", "The most common steroid: a membrane component and the starting material for bile salts, vitamin D and steroid hormones."),
("bile salts", "Amphipathic steroids made from cholesterol in the liver and released into the small intestine, where they emulsify fats for digestion."),
("lipoprotein", "A particle of lipids and protein that carries fats and cholesterol in the blood: chylomicrons, VLDL, LDL and HDL."),
("LDL, low-density lipoprotein", "The lipoprotein that carries cholesterol to the tissues; high LDL is linked to plaque in arteries (often called bad cholesterol)."),
("HDL, high-density lipoprotein", "The lipoprotein that carries cholesterol from the tissues back to the liver (often called good cholesterol)."),
("eicosanoids", "Signaling molecules made from the 20-carbon arachidonic acid: prostaglandins, thromboxanes and leukotrienes."),
("prostaglandins", "Eicosanoids that act as local hormones in inflammation, pain, fever and blood clotting; aspirin and ibuprofen block their synthesis by inhibiting COX."),
# ---------- Chapter 10: amino acids and proteins ----------
("amino acid", "A compound with an amino group and a carboxyl group; the 20 α-amino acids in proteins each have an –NH<sub>2</sub>, a –COOH, an H and a side chain (R group) on the same carbon."),
("α-carbon, alpha carbon", "In an amino acid, the carbon that holds the amino group, the carboxyl group, H and the side chain; next to a carbonyl in other compounds."),
("side chain, R group", "The part of an amino acid that differs from one amino acid to another and gives it its properties: nonpolar, polar neutral, acidic or basic."),
("essential amino acids", "Amino acids the body cannot make in sufficient amounts and must get from the diet."),
("zwitterion", "A molecule with both a positive and a negative charge but no net charge; amino acids exist as zwitterions (–NH<sub>3</sub><sup>+</sup> and –COO<sup>−</sup>) at physiological pH."),
("isoelectric point, pI", "The pH at which an amino acid or protein has no net charge; below the pI it is positive and above the pI it is negative."),
("peptide bond", "The amide bond that joins the carboxyl group of one amino acid to the amino group of the next."),
("peptide", "A short chain of amino acids joined by peptide bonds; dipeptides, tripeptides and so on."),
("polypeptide", "A long chain of amino acids joined by peptide bonds."),
("N-terminus, N-terminal", "The end of a peptide with the free amino group, written on the left."),
("C-terminus, C-terminal", "The end of a peptide with the free carboxyl group, written on the right."),
("primary structure", "The sequence of amino acids in a protein, held by peptide bonds."),
("secondary structure", "Regular folding of the protein backbone held by hydrogen bonds between peptide groups: the α helix and the β-pleated sheet."),
("α helix, alpha helix", "A secondary structure in which the polypeptide coils like a spring, held by hydrogen bonds between peptide groups four amino acids apart."),
("β-pleated sheet, beta-pleated sheet", "A secondary structure in which stretches of polypeptide lie side by side, held by hydrogen bonds between the strands."),
("tertiary structure", "The overall three-dimensional folding of a protein, held by side-chain interactions: hydrophobic interactions, hydrogen bonds, salt bridges and disulfide bonds."),
("quaternary structure", "The arrangement of two or more polypeptide subunits in a protein, such as the four chains of hemoglobin."),
("salt bridge", "An ionic attraction between a positively charged side chain (such as Lys) and a negatively charged side chain (such as Asp) in a protein."),
("hydrophobic interaction", "The clustering of nonpolar side chains together inside a protein, away from water."),
("fibrous protein", "A long, rope-like, water-insoluble structural protein, such as collagen, keratin and elastin."),
("globular protein", "A compact, rounded, water-soluble protein, such as hemoglobin, albumin and most enzymes."),
("collagen", "The most abundant protein in the body: a triple helix of polypeptide chains that gives strength to skin, tendons, bone, gums and the dentin of teeth."),
("hemoglobin", "The oxygen-carrying protein of red blood cells: four polypeptide chains, each with a heme group containing iron."),
("denaturation", "Loss of a protein's secondary, tertiary and quaternary structure (and its function) caused by heat, acids, bases, alcohol, heavy metals or agitation; the primary structure stays intact."),
("sickle cell disease, sickle-cell anemia", "An inherited disease in which one amino acid in hemoglobin (glutamate) is replaced by valine, so deoxygenated hemoglobin clumps and red cells take a sickle shape."),
# ---------- Chapter 11: enzymes and vitamins ----------
("enzyme", "A biological catalyst, almost always a protein, that speeds up a specific reaction by lowering its activation energy without being used up."),
("activation energy", "The minimum energy colliding molecules need to react; enzymes lower it."),
("substrate", "The molecule an enzyme acts on."),
("active site", "The pocket on an enzyme where the substrate binds and the reaction takes place."),
("enzyme–substrate complex, enzyme-substrate complex", "The temporary combination formed when the substrate binds the enzyme's active site."),
("lock-and-key model", "The model in which the active site has a fixed shape that fits only one substrate."),
("induced-fit model, induced fit", "The model in which the active site changes shape slightly to fit the substrate as it binds."),
("oxidoreductase", "An enzyme class that catalyzes oxidation–reduction reactions, such as dehydrogenases and oxidases."),
("transferase", "An enzyme class that moves a functional group from one molecule to another, such as kinases, aminotransferases and citrate synthase."),
("hydrolase", "An enzyme class that breaks bonds by adding water, such as lipases, proteases and amylases."),
("lyase", "An enzyme class that adds or removes groups across a double bond without water or ATP, such as fumarase and decarboxylases."),
("isomerase", "An enzyme class that rearranges atoms within a molecule."),
("ligase", "An enzyme class that joins two molecules using energy from ATP, such as DNA ligase."),
("optimum temperature", "The temperature at which an enzyme is most active (about 37 °C for human enzymes); above it the enzyme denatures."),
("optimum pH", "The pH at which an enzyme is most active, such as about 2 for pepsin and about 8 for trypsin."),
("saturation", "The point at which every active site is occupied, so adding more substrate no longer increases the rate."),
("enzyme inhibitor, inhibitor", "A substance that slows or stops an enzyme's activity."),
("competitive inhibitor, competitive inhibition", "An inhibitor that resembles the substrate and competes for the active site; it can be overcome by more substrate."),
("noncompetitive inhibitor, noncompetitive inhibition", "An inhibitor that binds somewhere other than the active site and changes the enzyme's shape; more substrate does not overcome it."),
("irreversible inhibitor, irreversible inhibition", "An inhibitor that bonds covalently to an enzyme and inactivates it permanently, such as aspirin, penicillin and nerve agents; heavy metals act this way too."),
("allosteric enzyme, allosteric regulation", "An enzyme whose activity is turned up or down when a regulator binds a site other than the active site."),
("feedback inhibition, feedback control", "Control of a pathway in which its end product inhibits an enzyme early in the pathway."),
("zymogen, proenzyme", "An inactive form of an enzyme that is activated by cutting off part of its chain, such as trypsinogen and pepsinogen."),
("covalent modification", "Turning an enzyme on or off by attaching or removing a group, usually a phosphate added by a protein kinase."),
("cofactor", "A non-protein helper that an enzyme needs to work: a metal ion or an organic coenzyme."),
("coenzyme", "An organic cofactor, usually made from a vitamin, such as NAD<sup>+</sup>, FAD and coenzyme A."),
("vitamin", "An organic compound needed in small amounts for normal health that the body cannot make in sufficient amounts."),
("water-soluble vitamins", "Vitamin C and the B vitamins: not stored, excreted in urine, needed regularly; most are coenzymes."),
("fat-soluble vitamins", "Vitamins A, D, E and K: absorbed with dietary fat and stored in fat and the liver, so excess can build up to toxic levels."),
("scurvy", "Vitamin C deficiency: weak collagen causes bleeding gums, loose teeth and poor wound healing."),
# ---------- Chapter 12: nucleic acids and protein synthesis ----------
("nucleic acid", "A polymer of nucleotides that stores and transmits genetic information: DNA and RNA."),
("nucleotide", "The monomer of nucleic acids: a nitrogen base, a five-carbon sugar and a phosphate group."),
("nucleoside", "A nitrogen base bonded to a five-carbon sugar, without a phosphate, such as adenosine."),
("nitrogen base", "One of the nitrogen-containing heterocyclic bases in nucleic acids: adenine, guanine, cytosine, thymine (DNA) and uracil (RNA)."),
("purine", "A base with a fused double ring: adenine (A) and guanine (G)."),
("pyrimidine", "A base with a single ring: cytosine (C), thymine (T) and uracil (U)."),
("deoxyribose, 2-deoxyribose", "The five-carbon sugar of DNA; it lacks the –OH on carbon 2′ that ribose has."),
("ribose", "The five-carbon sugar of RNA and ATP."),
("phosphodiester bond", "The phosphate link that joins the 3′ carbon of one nucleotide's sugar to the 5′ carbon of the next, forming the sugar–phosphate backbone."),
("DNA, deoxyribonucleic acid", "The nucleic acid that stores genetic information: two antiparallel strands of nucleotides wound into a double helix."),
("RNA, ribonucleic acid", "The single-stranded nucleic acid with ribose and uracil that carries out protein synthesis: mRNA, tRNA and rRNA."),
("double helix", "The structure of DNA: two strands coiled around each other, held together by hydrogen bonds between complementary base pairs."),
("complementary base pairs", "The base pairs held by hydrogen bonds in nucleic acids: A with T (or U in RNA), with two hydrogen bonds, and G with C, with three."),
("antiparallel", "Running in opposite directions: one DNA strand runs 5′ to 3′ and its partner runs 3′ to 5′."),
("replication", "The copying of DNA before cell division: each strand serves as the template for a new complementary strand."),
("DNA polymerase", "The enzyme that builds a new DNA strand by adding nucleotides complementary to the template, in the 5′ to 3′ direction."),
("transcription", "The synthesis of an RNA molecule from a DNA template."),
("translation", "The synthesis of a protein on a ribosome, using the codons of an mRNA to place amino acids in order."),
("central dogma", "The flow of genetic information: DNA is replicated, DNA is transcribed into RNA and RNA is translated into protein."),
("messenger RNA, mRNA", "The RNA that carries the genetic code from DNA to the ribosome."),
("transfer RNA, tRNA", "The RNA that brings a specific amino acid to the ribosome and matches its anticodon to a codon on the mRNA."),
("ribosomal RNA, rRNA", "The RNA that, with proteins, makes up the ribosome."),
("ribosome", "The particle of rRNA and protein where translation takes place."),
("codon", "A sequence of three mRNA bases that codes for one amino acid or a stop signal."),
("anticodon", "The three-base sequence on a tRNA that pairs with a complementary codon on the mRNA."),
("genetic code", "The set of codons and the amino acids they specify; it is nearly universal and degenerate (most amino acids have more than one codon)."),
("gene", "A segment of DNA that carries the instructions for one protein or RNA."),
("introns, exons", "Exons are the parts of a gene that are expressed; introns are the intervening sequences that are cut out of the pre-mRNA before translation."),
("mutation", "A change in the base sequence of DNA."),
("point mutation, substitution mutation", "A mutation in which one base is replaced by another; it may change one amino acid (missense), create a stop codon (nonsense) or have no effect (silent)."),
("frameshift mutation", "A mutation from inserting or deleting a base, which shifts the reading frame and changes every codon after it."),
("mutagen", "A physical or chemical agent that causes mutations, such as UV light, X-rays and certain chemicals."),
("recombinant DNA", "DNA made by combining genes from different sources, such as inserting the human insulin gene into a bacterial plasmid."),
("polymerase chain reaction, PCR", "A laboratory method that makes millions of copies of a DNA segment by repeated cycles of heating, primer binding and copying."),
("virus", "A small particle of DNA or RNA in a protein coat that can reproduce only inside a host cell."),
# ---------- Chapter 13: carbohydrate metabolism ----------
("metabolism", "All the chemical reactions in the body: catabolism plus anabolism."),
("catabolism, catabolic", "Reactions that break large molecules into smaller ones and release energy."),
("anabolism, anabolic", "Reactions that build large molecules from smaller ones and use energy."),
("ATP, adenosine triphosphate", "The energy carrier of the cell: hydrolysis of its terminal phosphate (ATP → ADP + P<sub>i</sub>) releases energy to drive reactions."),
("NAD+, NADH", "Nicotinamide adenine dinucleotide, a coenzyme made from niacin: NAD<sup>+</sup> accepts a hydride in oxidation reactions and becomes NADH, which carries the electrons to the electron transport chain."),
("FAD, FADH2", "Flavin adenine dinucleotide, a coenzyme made from riboflavin: FAD accepts two hydrogens when a C–C bond is oxidized to C=C and becomes FADH<sub>2</sub>."),
("coenzyme A, CoA", "A coenzyme made from pantothenic acid that carries acyl groups as thioesters, such as acetyl CoA."),
("acetyl CoA", "Acetyl coenzyme A: the two-carbon acetyl group carried as a thioester; the common product of carbohydrate, fat and amino acid breakdown, and the fuel of the citric acid cycle."),
("digestion", "The hydrolysis of food polymers into absorbable monomers: polysaccharides to monosaccharides, proteins to amino acids and fats to fatty acids and monoacylglycerols."),
("glycolysis", "The ten-step pathway in the cytosol that splits glucose into two pyruvate, with a net gain of 2 ATP and 2 NADH; it needs no oxygen."),
("pyruvate", "The three-carbon end product of glycolysis, CH<sub>3</sub>COCOO<sup>−</sup>: converted to acetyl CoA with oxygen, or to lactate (or ethanol in yeast) without it."),
("aerobic", "Requiring oxygen."),
("anaerobic", "Without oxygen."),
("lactate, lactic acid", "The product of reducing pyruvate when oxygen is low, which regenerates NAD<sup>+</sup> so glycolysis can continue; lactic acid from mouth bacteria dissolves tooth enamel."),
("fermentation", "Anaerobic breakdown of glucose that regenerates NAD<sup>+</sup>: lactate fermentation in muscle and bacteria, or alcoholic fermentation (ethanol and CO<sub>2</sub>) in yeast."),
("Cori cycle", "The cycle in which lactate from working muscle goes to the liver, is converted back to glucose and returns to the muscle."),
("glycogenesis", "The synthesis of glycogen from glucose, stimulated by insulin."),
("glycogenolysis", "The breakdown of glycogen to glucose (glucose 6-phosphate), stimulated by glucagon and epinephrine."),
("gluconeogenesis", "The synthesis of glucose from noncarbohydrate sources such as lactate, glycerol and most amino acids, mainly in the liver during fasting."),
("pentose phosphate pathway", "A pathway that oxidizes glucose 6-phosphate to make NADPH (for biosynthesis and protection against oxidation) and ribose 5-phosphate (for nucleotides)."),
("insulin", "The pancreatic hormone released when blood glucose is high; it signals cells to take up glucose and store it as glycogen and fat."),
("glucagon", "The pancreatic hormone released when blood glucose is low; it signals the liver to break down glycogen and make new glucose."),
("diabetes mellitus", "A disorder of high blood glucose caused by too little insulin (type 1) or poor response to insulin (type 2)."),
# ---------- Chapter 14: energy production ----------
("mitochondrion, mitochondria", "The cell organelle where the citric acid cycle, the electron transport chain and oxidative phosphorylation take place; the cell's power plant."),
("mitochondrial matrix", "The inner compartment of the mitochondrion, where the citric acid cycle and β-oxidation occur."),
("citric acid cycle, Krebs cycle, tricarboxylic acid cycle", "The eight-step cycle in the mitochondrial matrix that oxidizes the acetyl group of acetyl CoA to 2 CO<sub>2</sub>, producing 3 NADH, 1 FADH<sub>2</sub> and 1 GTP per turn."),
("oxaloacetate", "The four-carbon compound that combines with acetyl CoA to start the citric acid cycle and is regenerated at its end."),
("decarboxylation", "A reaction that removes a carboxyl group as CO<sub>2</sub>."),
("electron transport chain", "The series of protein complexes and carriers in the inner mitochondrial membrane that pass electrons from NADH and FADH<sub>2</sub> to O<sub>2</sub>, pumping H<sup>+</sup> as they go."),
("oxidative phosphorylation", "The synthesis of ATP using the energy of the electron transport chain."),
("chemiosmotic model, chemiosmosis", "The model in which the H<sup>+</sup> gradient built by the electron transport chain drives ATP synthesis as H<sup>+</sup> flows back through ATP synthase."),
("ATP synthase", "The enzyme in the inner mitochondrial membrane that makes ATP from ADP and P<sub>i</sub> as H<sup>+</sup> flows through it."),
("coenzyme Q, ubiquinone", "A mobile, lipid-soluble electron carrier in the electron transport chain that passes electrons from complexes I and II to complex III."),
("cytochrome", "An iron-containing (heme) protein that carries electrons in the electron transport chain, such as cytochrome c."),
("uncoupler", "A substance that lets H<sup>+</sup> leak back into the matrix without passing through ATP synthase, so the energy is released as heat instead of ATP, as in brown fat."),
("reactive oxygen species", "Highly reactive oxygen-containing molecules such as superoxide and hydrogen peroxide, formed in small amounts by the electron transport chain; antioxidants protect against them."),
# ---------- Chapter 15: lipid and protein metabolism ----------
("lipase", "An enzyme that hydrolyzes the ester bonds of triacylglycerols, such as pancreatic lipase in digestion and hormone-sensitive lipase in fat cells."),
("emulsification", "Breaking fat into tiny droplets, done by bile salts, so that water-soluble lipase can reach it."),
("chylomicron", "The large lipoprotein particle made in intestinal cells that carries dietary triacylglycerols into the lymph and blood."),
("adipose tissue", "Fat tissue: the body's store of triacylglycerols."),
("lipolysis, fat mobilization", "The hydrolysis of stored triacylglycerols into glycerol and fatty acids, stimulated by glucagon and epinephrine."),
("β-oxidation, beta oxidation", "The four-step spiral in the mitochondrial matrix that removes two carbons at a time from a fatty acid as acetyl CoA, producing 1 FADH<sub>2</sub> and 1 NADH per turn."),
("carnitine", "The carrier that shuttles fatty acyl groups across the inner mitochondrial membrane for β-oxidation."),
("ketone bodies", "Acetoacetate, β-hydroxybutyrate and acetone: made in the liver from excess acetyl CoA during fasting or uncontrolled diabetes, and used as fuel by the brain and muscle."),
("ketosis", "A raised level of ketone bodies in the blood; acetone gives the breath a fruity smell."),
("ketoacidosis, diabetic ketoacidosis", "A dangerous drop in blood pH caused by large amounts of acidic ketone bodies, most often in uncontrolled type 1 diabetes."),
("lipogenesis, fatty acid synthesis", "The synthesis of fatty acids from acetyl CoA in the cytosol, using NADPH; excess carbohydrate is stored as fat this way."),
("amino acid pool", "The free amino acids in the body available for building proteins and other nitrogen compounds or for energy."),
("nitrogen balance", "The difference between nitrogen taken in and nitrogen excreted; negative during starvation, illness and injury."),
("transamination", "The transfer of an amino group from an amino acid to an α-keto acid, catalyzed by an aminotransferase with vitamin B<sub>6</sub> (PLP)."),
("aminotransferase, transaminase", "An enzyme that catalyzes transamination; blood levels of ALT and AST rise when liver or heart cells are damaged."),
("oxidative deamination", "The removal of an amino group as ammonium ion, NH<sub>4</sub><sup>+</sup>, while the carbon is oxidized, as when glutamate becomes α-ketoglutarate."),
("urea cycle", "The liver pathway that converts toxic ammonium ion and CO<sub>2</sub> into urea for excretion in urine."),
("urea", "H<sub>2</sub>N–CO–NH<sub>2</sub>: the water-soluble, nontoxic compound in which the body excretes nitrogen; measured as blood urea nitrogen (BUN)."),
("glucogenic amino acid", "An amino acid whose carbon skeleton can be converted to glucose."),
("ketogenic amino acid", "An amino acid whose carbon skeleton is converted to acetyl CoA or ketone bodies, not glucose."),
("phenylketonuria, PKU", "An inherited disorder in which phenylalanine cannot be converted to tyrosine, so it builds up; newborns are screened and treated with a low-phenylalanine diet."),
]
